Rae (AE): Elena reports their POC frontend intermittently crashes when rendering streamed completions. Browser shows `Unexpected token \u0000` during JSON.parse.
Elena (Client): repro: open SSE to /poc/stream, after a few events one event fails parsing and the UI blows up. No 5xx from origin.
Marcus (SE): please run a raw capture: `curl -N -v https://your-poc/stream > s.bin` then `xxd -g1 s.bin | sed -n '1,120p'` and paste the area around the failure.
Elena (Client): pasted hexdump excerpt: `... 7d 0a 0a 64 61 74 61 3a 20 7b 22id\":\"msgA\"7d 00 0a 0a`  there's a 00 byte right after the JSON `}`.
Sam (Oncall): 0x00 in the event payload will break JSON.parse if the client concatenates chunks. Typical culprits: CDN/edge or reverse proxy buffer padding, or upstream writes that use fixed-size buffers. What sits in front of your app?
Elena (Client): Cloudflare in front of nginx; then our app.
Harold (Eng): we've seen Cloudflare/edge or nginx re-chunking insert NULs when buffering or doing HTTP/2->HTTP/1 conversions. Two-prong fix: infra (disable buffering / ensure chunked) and client hardening (accumulate until event delimiter).
Marcus (SE): infra quick checklist to try on the stream location: `proxy_buffering off; proxy_request_buffering off; gzip off; proxy_http_version 1.1; chunked_transfer_encoding on; sendfile off; proxy_read_timeout 3600;` Also try bypassing Cloudflare to see if edge is source.
Elena (Client): bypassed CF to origin -> hexdump clean. So Cloudflare was introducing it in our case.
deploy-bot: Deployed `poc/stream-bypass-test` to staging (build #203). :white_check_mark:
Marcus (SE): short-term client workaround: do not parse per-chunk. Accumulate until you see the SSE blank-line separator `\n\n`, strip any NULs, then JSON.parse. Minimal snippet (browser/fetch):
```
let buf = '';
const reader = resp.body.getReader();
async function pump(){
  while(true){
    const {value,done} = await reader.read();
    if(done) break;
    buf += new TextDecoder().decode(value, {stream:true});
    let i;
    while((i = buf.indexOf('\n\n')) !== -1){
      const evt = buf.slice(0,i).trim();
      buf = buf.slice(i+2);
      const data = evt.split('\n').filter(l=>l.startsWith('data:')).map(l=>l.replace(/^data:\s*/,'')).join('\n');
      const clean = data.replace(/\u0000/g,'');
      try { JSON.parse(clean); } catch (e) { console.warn('sse parse fail', e, clean); }
    }
  }
}
```
Sam (Oncall): infra action: ask Cloudflare to disable HTTP/2 response buffering for that route or put it on a bypass rule. For nginx confirm you return chunked transfer and do not set Content-Length for the stream.
Harold (Eng): on Redwood side we can flip a tenant runtime flag `sse_strict=true` that forces immediate flush per event (avoids internal buffer reuse that may leak zeros). Low risk for POC; will log telemetry if we see padding. Faye please confirm we can toggle for this customer.
Faye (Presales): fine to toggle for troubleshooting; note it in the POC report.
Elena (Client): applied CF bypass + added client accumulator and NUL strip. We've run 8 long streams with no crashes. Much better.
Marcus (SE): follow-ups: 1) Sam/Harold to draft a short ops blurb with nginx + Cloudflare guidance for SSE routes, 2) Marcus/Faye to add the client workaround and runtime toggle to the POC wrap-up. Please add links when done.
Sam (Oncall): I'll scan our edge logs for other domains showing 0x00 padding and open infra tickets if found.
Harold (Eng): I'll open an internal ticket to consider defaulting `sse_strict=true` for new POCs and to collect padding telemetry per tenant.
Rae (AE): thanks team  great swoop. Closing once docs are added to the POC notes. :tada:
